#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# comap

#% text
# `comap(fs, x)` applies each function in `fs` to `x`, keeping the head of `fs`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `comap([f, g], x)` is `[f(x), g(x)]`; `comap(f + g, x)` is `f(x) + g(x)`.

#% item
# An association of functions gives an association of values.

#% item
# `comap(fs)` is the operator form.

#% item
# `comap(fs, x, levels)` applies the parts of `fs` at the levels (a level specification as for `level`).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
comap([f, g, h], x)
#% output
#= [f(x), g(x), h(x)]

#% cell
comap([sin, cos, tan], pi/3)
#% output
#= [(1/2)*3^(1/2), 1/2, 3^(1/2)]

#% cell
comap([mean, median, max], [6, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 0])
#% output
#= [23/7, 4, 6]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# At level 2:

#% cell
comap([[sin, cos], [tan]], 0, [2])
#% output
#= [[0, 1], [0]]

#% cell
comap(assoc("a" -> f, "b" -> g), x)
#% output
#= assoc("a" -> f(x), "b" -> g(x))

#% cell
comap(f + g, x)
#% output
#= f(x) + g(x)

#% cell
comap([sin, cos])(pi/6)
#% output
#= [1/2, (1/2)*3^(1/2)]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `comap_apply`, `map`, `through`
